steps.body:
A `std::stack<T>` from `<stack>` is last in, first out: the last thing you push is the first thing you get back, like a pile of plates.

```cpp
std::cout << std::boolalpha;
std::stack<char> st;
st.push('a');
st.push('b');
std::cout << st.top() << '\n';    // b, the newest element
std::cout << st.size() << '\n';   // 2
std::cout << st.empty() << '\n';  // false
```

Only the top is visible: a stack has no indexing and no range-for loop.

> Gotcha: `pop()` returns `void`. It removes the top but does not hand it to you, so read `top()` first and copy it into a value, then pop:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    std::stack<char> st;
    st.push('a');
    st.push('b');
    {{code}}
    std::cout << c << ' ' << st.size() << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
char c = st.top();
st.pop();
```

`top()` returns a reference into the stack, and `pop()` destroys that element, so a `const auto&` taken before `pop()` dangles. Calling `top()` or `pop()` on an empty stack is undefined behavior, so check `empty()` first.

A classic use is checking brackets. Push every opener. On each closer, the stack must not be empty and its top must be the matching opener. At the end the stack must be empty, or some opener was never closed.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <stack>
#include <string>

bool isBalanced(const std::string& text) {
    std::stack<char> openers;
    for (char c : text) {
        if (c == '(' || c == '[' || c == '{') {
            openers.push(c);
        } else {
            if (openers.empty()) {
                return false;
            }
            char open = openers.top();
            openers.pop();
            if ((c == ')' && open != '(') || (c == ']' && open != '[') || (c == '}' && open != '{')) {
                return false;
            }
        }
    }
    return openers.empty();
}

int main() {
    const std::string word = "drawer";
    std::stack<char> letters;
    for (char c : word) {
        letters.push(c);
    }
    std::cout << letters.size() << '\n';
    std::cout << letters.top() << '\n';

    std::string reversed = "";
    while (!letters.empty()) {
        char c = letters.top();
        letters.pop();
        reversed += c;
    }
    std::cout << reversed << '\n';

    std::cout << (isBalanced("{[()]}") ? "balanced" : "not balanced") << '\n';
    std::cout << (isBalanced("{[(])}") ? "balanced" : "not balanced") << '\n';
    return 0;
}

